  "query_preview": ".. Our region is currently under the influence of dry\nsouthwesterly flow courtesy of a longwave troughing pattern off \nthe west coast. This trough is expected to deepen along the coast \non Friday as a northern stream cutoff low digs southward, \nintroducing more moist, southerly flow aloft that will tap into \nwhat is currently Tropical Storm Vance. Concurrently, a shortwave\nis expected to eject northeastward through the region around the \neastern periphery of the parent trough, introducing modest amounts\nof instability that will aid in the development of widely\nscattered showers and thunderstorms throughout the day. Storms are\nexpected to develop mid to late morning first across southwest\nUtah, with coverage expanding northward through the day as\nmoisture fills into the region. Particularly across southwest\nUtah, where deepest moisture and instability is maximized, flash\nflooding will be a risk. Current high-res guidance shows a \nbullseye of heaviest precipitation concentrated around Zion NP and\nthe southern mountains, with a 75% chance of accumulations \nexceeding 0.3-0.8\" Friday into Saturday morning, and a 25% chance\nfor accumulations to exceed 0.8-1.3\" in these areas. Elsewhere,\naccumulations are expected to be on the lighter side, between a\nfew hundredths of an inch upwards of 0.25\". \n\nThere will be a relative lull in storm activity on Sunday as\nsubsidence behind the exiting shortwave fills in. Given some\nlingering moisture, there is some potential for some more isolated\nshower activity in the afternoon and evening, but overall\nconditions remain dry for holiday festivities.\n\nOn Sunday, the aforementioned northern stream cutoff low will\nphase with a Pacific low and shift inland. Confidence is\nincreasing for this system to bring widespread moderate to heavy\nprecipitation between Sunday and Monday morning. Two distinct \nareas of heaviest precipitation are evident in recent guidance: \none across southwest Utah, attributed to the associated \natmospheric river that will bring deep moisture and instability \nacross southern Utah, and one across northern Utah, attributed to \na period of heavy precipitation accompanying a cold front passage \non Sunday. QPF amounts have increased overall, with even low-end \nguidance indicating likely chances (75%+) for widespread \nprecipitation accumulations between 0.5-1\" between Sunday and \nMonday morning. Accumulations across southwest Utah, the Wasatch \nFront, and all Utah mountains will likely see accumulations closer\nto 1.5-2\" with localized areas seeing upwards of 3\".\n\nThe cold front associated with the northern stream trough will\nusher in a much cooler airmass late Sunday into Monday morning.\nSnow levels will gradually decrease from 9500 feet late Sunday to\naround 6500-7000 feet early Monday. As such, accumulating mountain\nsnow is expected through this period, with generally 3-6 inches\nexpected with upwards of 12-18 inches along the higher elevations.\n\nDrier conditions quickly build in on Monday as the weather system\nexits to our east, with much cooler conditions expected in its\nwake. High pressure is expected to prevail across the region\nthrough at least the middle half of next week.",
